## Proof

Let the four numbers be $x_1,x_2,x_3,x_4$, with
$$e_1=\sum x_i=9,\qquad \sum x_i^2=21,\qquad e_2=\sum_{i<j}x_ix_j=\frac{e_1^2-\sum x_i^2}{2}=\frac{81-21}{2}=30.$$

**Pairings.** Consider the three ways to split $\{1,2,3,4\}$ into two pairs, and set
$$d_1=x_1x_2-x_3x_4,\quad d_2=x_1x_3-x_2x_4,\quad d_3=x_1x_4-x_2x_3.$$
For each pairing we may choose which pair is called $(a,b)$ and which $(c,d)$, so the achievable value of $ab-cd$ for that pairing is $|d_i|$. Thus it suffices to prove $\max_i|d_i|\ge 2$.

**An identity.** Let $e_3=\sum_{i<j<k}x_ix_jx_k$ and $e_4=x_1x_2x_3x_4$. We compute $\sum d_i^2$.

Each squared pair-product $(x_ix_j)^2$ appears in exactly one $d_i^2$, and each $d_i^2$ contributes a cross term $-2e_4$, so
$$d_1^2+d_2^2+d_3^2=\Bigl(\sum_{i<j}(x_ix_j)^2\Bigr)-6e_4.$$
Writing $T=\sum_{i<j}(x_ix_j)^2$, we have $e_2^2=T+2U$ where $U=\sum x_ix_j\,x_kx_l$ over the $\binom{6}{2}=15$ pairs of distinct pair-products. These 15 split into the 3 *disjoint* pairings (each contributing $e_4$) and 12 *sharing-one-index* pairs. The latter sum to
$$\sum_{\text{triples }\{i,j,k\}} x_ix_jx_k(x_i+x_j+x_k)=e_1e_3-4e_4=9e_3-4e_4,$$
so $U=3e_4+(9e_3-4e_4)=9e_3-e_4$, and $T=900-2(9e_3-e_4)=900-18e_3+2e_4$. Hence
$$\boxed{d_1^2+d_2^2+d_3^2=900-18e_3-4e_4=900-2(9e_3+2e_4).}\tag{1}$$

So it is enough to prove

$$\boxed{9e_3+2e_4\le 444.}\tag{2}$$

**Proving (2).** Consider the set $R$ of all $(e_3,e_4)$ realized by real $x_i$ with $e_1=9,\ e_2=30$. It is compact (each $x_i\in[\tfrac32,3]$, since $(9-x_i)^2\le 3(21-x_i^2)\iff x_i\in[\tfrac32,3]$). The function $9e_3+2e_4$ is linear and nonconstant on $R$, so its maximum is attained on $\partial R$.

A point of $\partial R$ corresponds to the quartic $Q(t)=t^4-9t^3+30t^2-e_3t+e_4$ being at the transition between "all roots real" and "some root non-real", which happens exactly when $Q$ has a multiple real root, i.e. **two of the $x_i$ are equal**. (This is the standard *uvw* principle.)

Set $x_1=x_2=a$, $x_3=b$, $x_4=c$. From $2a+b+c=9$ and $2a^2+b^2+c^2=21$:
$$b+c=9-2a,\qquad bc=\frac{(9-2a)^2-(21-2a^2)}{2}=3a^2-18a+30.$$
Reality of $b,c$ requires $(b+c)^2-4bc\ge0$, i.e.
$$(9-2a)^2-4(3a^2-18a+30)\ge0\iff 8a^2-36a+39\le0\iff a\in\Bigl[\tfrac{9-\sqrt3}{4},\,\tfrac{9+\sqrt3}{4}\Bigr].\tag{3}$$

Now
$$e_3=a^2(b+c)+2abc=a^2(9-2a)+2a(3a^2-18a+30)=4a^3-27a^2+60a,$$
$$e_4=a^2bc=a^2(3a^2-18a+30)=3a^4-18a^3+30a^2,$$
so
$$9e_3+2e_4=6a^4-183a^2+540a.$$
A direct factorization gives
$$444-(9e_3+2e_4)=444-6a^4+183a^2-540a=-(a-2)^2(6a^2+24a-111).\tag{4}$$

The quadratic $6a^2+24a-111$ has roots $\frac{-4\pm3\sqrt{10}}{2}$; the positive root is $\frac{-4+3\sqrt{10}}{2}$. We claim it lies to the right of the interval (3):
$$\frac{9+\sqrt3}{4}<\frac{-4+3\sqrt{10}}{2}\iff 17+\sqrt3<6\sqrt{10}\iff (17+\sqrt3)^2<360\iff 34\sqrt3<68\iff \sqrt3<2,$$
which is true. The negative root is plainly below $\frac{9-\sqrt3}{4}$. Hence $6a^2+24a-111<0$ throughout the interval (3), and (4) yields
$$444-(9e_3+2e_4)\ge0,$$
proving (2). (Equality holds only at $a=2$, giving $(x_1,x_2,x_3,x_4)$ a permutation of $(2,2,2,3)$.)

**Conclusion.** From (1) and (2),
$$d_1^2+d_2^2+d_3^2\ge 900-2\cdot444=12.$$
Therefore $\max_i d_i^2\ge \tfrac{12}{3}=4$, so some $|d_i|\ge2$. Choosing the corresponding pairing and labeling the pair with the larger product as $(a,b)$ and the other as $(c,d)$, we obtain
$$ab-cd=|d_i|\ge2.$$

